-Also called: 3D Simplex Noise, Gradient Noise, Perlin Noise Implementation, Perlin Simplex Noise, Stefan Gustavson Simplex Noise, snoise
+Also called: 3D Simplex Noise, SNoise, Simplex Noise 3D
 
 ## Overview
-Simplex Noise is a gradient-based procedural noise function that generates smooth, continuous scalar fields by mapping input coordinates to a skewed simplex lattice (triangles in 2D, tetrahedra in 3D, pentachora in 4D) and summing weighted dot products of gradient vectors [S1, S4, S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of directional artifacts and high computational complexity inherent in the original integer-lattice Perlin Noise by utilizing a simplex grid, which provides a more even distribution of grid points and fewer gradient contributions per sample [S4, unofficial]. Unlike standard Perlin Noise which uses a square grid and requires four gradient evaluations in 2D, Simplex Noise 2D uses a triangular grid and requires only three gradient evaluations, lowering computational complexity and eliminating undesired directional artifacts [S4, unofficial]. It scales to higher dimensions ($n$) with complexity $O(n^2)$ compared to $O(n 2^n)$ for classic noise, making it easier to implement in hardware and suitable for higher-dimensional volume rendering [S11, unofficial].
+Simplex Noise is a gradient-based procedural noise function that generates smooth, continuous scalar fields by mapping input coordinates to a skewed simplex lattice (triangles in 2D, tetrahedra in 3D, pentachora in 4D) and summing weighted dot products of gradient vectors [S1][S2, S4, S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of directional artifacts and high computational complexity inherent in the original integer-lattice Perlin Noise by utilizing a simplex grid, which provides a more even distribution of grid points and fewer gradient contributions per sample [S1][S2, unofficial][S11]. Unlike standard Perlin Noise which uses a square grid and requires four gradient evaluations in 2D, Simplex Noise 2D uses a triangular grid and requires only three gradient evaluations, lowering computational complexity and eliminating undesired directional artifacts [S2, unofficial][S11]. It scales to higher dimensions ($n$) with complexity $O(n^2)$ compared to $O(n 2^n)$ for classic noise, making it easier to implement in hardware and suitable for higher-dimensional volume rendering [S11].
 
